<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Masovia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Masovia is a historical region of east-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>, centred first on Płock and later on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>. From 1138 it was a separate Piast duchy, often divided among branches of the dynasty, until it was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> in 1526. Its numerous petty nobility was characteristic of the region, and Warsaw became the royal capital in 1596.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/masovia.jpg" alt="Masovia" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aquarius 24 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P%C5%82ock_panorama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Masovia</b>, on the middle Vistula with its centre at Plock, was given to Boleslaw IV the Curly under the testament of Boleslaw III in 1138.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-masovia">Duchy of Masovia</a></li><li><blockquote>For two centuries Plock was sometimes reunited with the rest of <b>Masovia</b> and sometimes ruled as a separate duchy.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-plock">Duchy of Plock</a></li><li><blockquote>Siemowit III reunited most of <b>Masovia</b> in the 1370s and issued laws for it, but it was divided again among his heirs, and Rawa was ruled by the Siemowit and Wladyslaw lines of Plock.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-rawa">Duchy of Rawa</a></li><li><blockquote>The land of Lubawa lay in the south-west of Prussia, next to the Chełmno land and <b>Masovia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lubavians">Lubavians</a></li><li><blockquote>Like other Prussian groups they emerged as a distinct territory in the Viking Age, with farming villages and hillforts and contacts with Pomerania and <b>Masovia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/pomesanians">Pomesanians</a></li><li><blockquote>After that state collapsed in the 1340s Belz was contested by Poland and the Grand Duchy of Lithuania, then from 1388 held as a fief by the dukes of <b>Masovia</b>, beginning with Siemowit IV.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-belz">Principality of Belz</a></li></ul></section>
